QUTEBROWSER WORKSPACE

- [x] Make browser, terminal, and filesystem first-class tab types in the same qutebrowser window
- [x] Keep browser tabs as the default tab/content type
- [ ] Add native terminal/shell tabs usable for interactive programs such as nvim
- [ ] Add native cross-platform filesystem-manager tabs for Linux, Windows, and macOS
- [ ] Make all three tab types work with normal tab actions: focus, close, move, duplicate where applicable, and session restore where safe

OPEN COMMAND

- [x] Add application selectors to :open:
      -at  terminal
      -ab  browser
      -af  filesystem
- [x] Allow selectors to combine with existing :open flags
      example: :open -t -at
- [x] Preserve all existing qutebrowser :open flag meanings
- [x] Use browser when no application selector is supplied
- [x] Reject conflicting application selectors cleanly

SPLITS

- [x] Add vertical and horizontal pane splitting inside a qutebrowser window
- [x] Allow every pane to contain browser, terminal, or filesystem tabs
- [x] Allow mixed layouts, e.g. browser + terminal or filesystem + browser
- [x] Keep panes integrated with existing qutebrowser focus and command handling
- [x] Add pane focus, close, resize, and move commands
- [ ] Preserve pane layout in sessions where possible

KEYBINDS

- [x] hh opens the command line with a horizontal/side-by-side split selector for browser, filesystem, or terminal
- [x] vv opens the command line with a vertical/stacked split selector for browser, filesystem, or terminal
- [x] Shift+O keeps opening :open -t, now compatible with -at/-ab/-af

TERMINAL

- [x] Use the platform's real shell and PTY/ConPTY backend
- [x] Linux/macOS: PTY-based shell sessions
- [x] Windows: ConPTY-based shell sessions
- [ ] Support interactive TUI applications, colors, resize, mouse input, clipboard, and UTF-8
- [x] Inherit qutebrowser working directory/environment sensibly

FILESYSTEM

- [x] Native keyboard-driven file manager tab
- [ ] Cross-platform path, drive, volume, symlink, and permission handling
- [x] Open files/directories into appropriate qutebrowser workspace tabs/actions
- [x] Integrate filesystem cwd with terminal tabs

ARCHITECTURE

- [x] Introduce a generic workspace/tab-content abstraction instead of assuming every tab is a web page
- [ ] Keep existing browser-tab implementation behind a browser content adapter
- [x] Add terminal and filesystem content adapters without embedding them in web pages
- [x] Build pane/split management above the content abstraction
- [x] Keep existing qutebrowser behavior unchanged when workspace features are unused
- [ ] Add unit/integration tests for commands, tab types, pane lifecycle, session handling, and all three operating systems
